Outcome: DONE -- fix verified, one commit on agent/fix-issue-5460 (base fc9a6d9a), one file changed (+52/-9).
Out-of-range ctc_loss targets are now caught where they are used, by SYCL_KERNEL_ASSERT inside get_target_prime() (the choke point for 7 of the 8 target reads) and at the one read that bypasses the helper -- the atomic-write index in CTCLossBackwardCollectNonblankKernelFunctor.
Points a reviewer should weigh:

The failure mode is a process abort, not a RuntimeError. This issue's "Expected" section asks for a catchable error, and a device assert cannot give you one. A host-side check could, but it needs the targets copied to host plus a full device sync on every ctc_loss call -- which is why upstream's own fix (pytorch/pytorch#197799, still open) gates check_target_values on targets.device().is_cpu() and leaves accelerators unguarded. The trade taken here is a hard, self-describing failure instead of silent garbage and a lost device; it diverges from CUDA in the safe direction. If a catchable error is required instead, this is the decision to revisit.
No test is added. A device assert aborts the pytest worker, so it cannot be asserted on from inside the suite -- the same reason upstream's target-value test is CPU-only and the comparable guards in this repo (LossNLLKernel, Indexing, ScatterGatherKernels) are untested. The reproducer used for verification is not part of the commit.
Adjacent bug left alone, since it is outside this fix: the alpha and beta kernels read input_lengths_[b], target_lengths_[b] and tg_batch_offsets_[b] before the b >= batch_size_ guard.
