Two test cases (test_memory_stats_xpu, test_memory_stats_multigpu_xpu) fail from one cause: torch.xpu.memory_stats() does not report num_sync_all_streams, num_device_alloc, num_device_free.
Automated by issue-triage.



Reproduce
Nightly wheel 2.16.0.dev20261028+xpu on PVC (Intel Data Center GPU Max 1550, 2 devices), test file from pytorch/pytorch origin/main @ 3afdbd3b95e. Both cases carry @skipXPUIf(True, ...) pointing at this issue, so the markers were removed in-place for the run and the file reverted afterwards.



Test case
Verdict
Observed




test_multigpu.py::TestMultiGPUDeviceXPU::test_memory_stats_xpu
REPRODUCED
AssertionError: -1 not greater than or equal to 0


test_multigpu.py::TestMultiGPUDeviceXPU::test_memory_stats_multigpu_xpu
NOT RUNNABLE HERE
skipped by the pre-existing @unittest.skipIf(IS_LINUX, ...) for pytorch/pytorch#129860



Direct confirmation of the gap: torch.xpu.memory_stats() returns no num_sync_all_streams / num_device_alloc / num_device_free key at all, so the test's .get(key, -1) yields -1.
Automated by fix-reproduce.



Root Cause
Analyzed at pytorch origin/main@3afdbd3b95e.
torch.xpu.memory_stats() is built by THXPModule_memoryStats in torch/csrc/xpu/Module.cpp:199, which copies only the nine StatArray groups out of DeviceStats into the returned dict (Module.cpp:231-241). The scalar event counters that c10::CachingDeviceAllocator::DeviceStats declares -- num_sync_all_streams, num_device_alloc, num_device_free (c10/core/CachingDeviceAllocator.h:55-63) -- are never put in the dict, so the test's memory_stats(device).get("num_sync_all_streams", -1) returns its -1 default and assertGreaterEqual(num_sync_1, 0) fails.
The counters are missing at the source as well, not just at the Python boundary: c10/xpu/XPUCachingAllocator.cpp increments only num_alloc_retries (line 1025). The three sites that should bump them have no counter at all -- alloc_block (line 1055-1069, the sycl::aligned_alloc_device path), map_block (line 909-923, expandable segments), release_block (line 1132-1144), unmap_block (line 1206-1227) and synchronize_and_free_events (line 1075) -- and resetAccumulatedStats (line 1807) therefore resets only num_alloc_retries.
CUDA keeps the same three counters next to exactly the corresponding trace records: c10/cuda/CUDACachingAllocator.cpp:4450 (alloc_block, before SEGMENT_ALLOC), :3880 (map_block, before SEGMENT_MAP), :4593 (release_block, before SEGMENT_FREE), :4713 (unmap_block, before SEGMENT_UNMAP), :4759 (synchronize_and_free_events), reset at :2832-2834, and exposes them in torch/csrc/cuda/Module.cpp:629-631. The XPU allocator has a structurally identical set of functions, so the fix is to mirror those five increments, extend resetAccumulatedStats, add the three dict entries in the XPU Module.cpp, document them in torch/xpu/memory.py, and drop the two @skipXPUIf(True, ...) markers in test/test_multigpu.py that currently point at this issue.

Verdict: IMPLEMENTING -- a bounded CUDA-parity change entirely inside pytorch
Target repo: pytorch
Domain: upstream-pytorch

Automated by fix-root-cause.



Implement Result

Target repo: pytorch
Analyzed at: pytorch@3afdbd3b95e
What I changed: c10/xpu/XPUCachingAllocator.cpp (+9) bumps the three counters at the five sites that already emit the matching trace record, and clears them in resetAccumulatedStats; torch/csrc/xpu/Module.cpp (+3) puts them in the memory_stats dict; torch/xpu/memory.py (+9) documents them; test/test_multigpu.py (-2) drops the two now-stale @skipXPUIf markers.
Why: the counters were absent both at the Python boundary (torch/csrc/xpu/Module.cpp:233 copied only StatArray groups) and at the source (c10/xpu/XPUCachingAllocator.cpp:1025 tracked only num_alloc_retries). Placement follows CUDA, which keeps each counter adjacent to the trace record for the same event: c10/cuda/CUDACachingAllocator.cpp:4450 (alloc_block/SEGMENT_ALLOC), :3880 (map_block/SEGMENT_MAP), :4593 (release_block/SEGMENT_FREE), :4713 (unmap_block/SEGMENT_UNMAP), :4759 (synchronize_and_free_events), reset at :2832-2834, exposed at torch/csrc/cuda/Module.cpp:629-631.
Scope note: only the three counters this issue names. XPU still lacks num_ooms, num_oom_rejections, max_split_size and the oversize_* stats CUDA reports, and still does not expose the num_alloc_retries it already tracks -- left alone rather than widened into this fix.
Skip added: no (two stale ones removed)
Ready for verify: yes

