    xpu=[inf, inf, inf, inf, -inf]
    cpu=[2.003902068840785e+34, 2.005462863642316e+29, 2.0032011142543966e+19, inf, -6.997431313259826e+32]
  MISMATCH[signature] dtype=torch.float16 s=1e-39 mode=None
    xpu=[inf, nan, nan, inf, -inf]
    cpu=[inf, 0.0, 0.0, inf, -inf]

Automated by fix-reproduce.



Root Cause
Analyzed at torch-xpu-ops@36aa2394 (origin/main), pytorch origin/main.
All three XPU division kernels apply the "multiply by the host-computed reciprocal" optimization to a CPU scalar divisor with no guard on the reciprocal itself. src/ATen/native/xpu/sycl/BinaryDivTrueKernel.cpp:42 computes inv_b = opmath_t(1.0) / iter.scalar_value<opmath_t>(2) and then runs a MulFunctor kernel; src/ATen/native/xpu/sycl/BinaryDivTruncKernel.cpp:57 does the same into DivTruncScalarFunctor (sycl::trunc(a * inv_b_)); src/ATen/native/xpu/sycl/BinaryDivFloorKernel.cpp:97 into DivFloorWithScalarFunctor, whose div = (a_ - mod) * inv_b_ at line 51 is the multiply. For fp32, bf16 and fp16 the accumulate type is float, so a divisor around 1e-39 makes 1 / b overflow to inf (and a divisor near float max makes it subnormal). Multiplying by inf maps every finite element to +-inf, and 0 * inf to NaN, which is the exact pattern observed. float64 is unaffected because 1 / 1e-39 is representable in double. The in-code comments on those branches sanction losing "one bit of precision"; total loss of the value is outside that.
The bug is not new -- the reciprocal branch is original XPU code and these files have not been touched since #4099. What is new is only the report: pytorch/pytorch#201302 ("[CUDA] Divide by CPU scalars whose reciprocal overflows, and use true division for trunc") proposed the equivalent CUDA fix and was closed unmerged by its author within hours, with no review (pytorch/pytorch#201302 closed 2026-10-18, CI green, zero reviews). Its design is still the right reference: a host-side is_usable_reciprocal(inv_b) helper (inv_b == 0 || std::isnormal(inv_b)) in aten/src/ATen/native/cuda/BinaryInternal.h, used to fall back to real division. Note also pytorch/pytorch#191495, closed not planned, where a maintainer ruled that the reciprocal path's one-bit divergence from CPU is within expected accuracy -- that rationale is explicitly about one bit and does not cover inf/NaN for a finite quotient, which is what this issue reports.
Fix strategy: add is_usable_reciprocal to src/ATen/native/xpu/sycl/BinaryInternal.h and, in each of the three kernels, fall back to real division in the accumulate type when the guard rejects inv_b. Measured CPU reference semantics are "divide in opmath with the divisor held at opmath precision, then narrow", so the fallbacks keep opmath_t / accscalar_t rather than adopting upstream's high_prec_t (double) -- for fp32 a double divide then narrow does not reproduce CPU bit-for-bit, while an opmath_t divide does. The guard is host-side and the fast path is untouched, so there is no cost in the common case. Out of scope deliberately: the separate trunc issue that PR also addressed (a one-ulp error in a * (1/b) can move trunc by a whole integer), which this issue's oracle does not flag.

Verdict: IMPLEMENTING -- defect is in XPU-owned kernels in this repo, mechanism and fix are both local and small.
Target repo: torch-xpu-ops
Domain: xpu-kernel


[!NOTE]
Reviewer judgement call: CUDA has the same unguarded reciprocal today and the upstream fix attempt was closed, so this patch makes XPU match CPU and diverge from CUDA. That direction is defensible -- inf for a finite quotient is not the one-bit tradeoff upstream signed off on, and XPU tensor / tensor is already finite here -- but it is a policy call that belongs to a human, which is why it is flagged rather than buried.

