Target repo: torch-xpu-ops
Analyzed at: torch-xpu-ops@36aa2394
What I changed: BinaryInternal.h (+17) adds the host-side is_usable_reciprocal guard and its complex overload; BinaryDivTrueKernel.cpp (+16/-1) keeps b and falls back to a DivFunctor kernel; BinaryDivTruncKernel.cpp (+21/-2) adds DivTruncByScalarFunctor (trunc(acc(a) / b)) and the guard; BinaryDivFloorKernel.cpp (+23/-1) adds DivFloorByScalarFunctor (c10::div_floor_floating(acc(a), b)), the guard, and the BinaryInternal.h include.
Why: each of the three kernels multiplied by a host-computed 1/b with nothing checking that the reciprocal survived; the guard sends the overflowed/subnormal case down a real division in the accumulate type instead. The fallbacks hold the divisor at opmath_t/accscalar_t rather than at double because that is what reproduces CPU: for a float32 tensor, dividing in float32 matches torch.div on CPU bit-for-bit over all 27 probed cases, while dividing in float64 and narrowing does not. The fast path and the tensor-tensor path are untouched.
